Title: The toolbar control is focusable but may not be in Zotero's tab route
Created: 2026-09-14
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-14T05:30Z claude created
2026-09-14T05:31Z claude note Filed from the decorrelated review of PR #554, which raised it as a structural concern the PR neither tested nor discussed. The reviewer could not confirm it live for the same reason PR #554's own probe could not read tab order — no X focus on the test display, and no xdotool on the host — so this is filed as an open question with the evidence that motivates it, not as a confirmed defect. Filing it rather than fixing it because the remedy is not obvious: it may be an attribute, or it may be an entry in a Zotero map this add-on has no business writing into.

--- body ---
## Context

PR #554 (ticket 0769) fixed a real defect: the sitter's toolbar control is a XUL
`toolbarbutton`, which is not focusable by default and carried no `tabindex`, so
`button.focus()` did nothing and the panel could be opened only with a mouse.
Adding `tabindex="0"` fixed that, demonstrated both in the DOM and — causally,
by removing the attribute on a live window and watching the state vanish — over
AT-SPI, where the control now reports `focusable`.

The review then asked the question the fix does not answer: **is the control
reachable by actually pressing Tab?**

## The evidence

Read out of the shipped `omni.ja` of Zotero 10.0.1:

- Every button in `zotero-items-toolbar` — `zotero-tb-add`, `zotero-tb-lookup`,
  `zotero-tb-attachment-add`, `zotero-tb-note-add` — carries `tabindex="-1"`,
  i.e. Zotero deliberately takes its own toolbar buttons OUT of the natural tab
  order.
- `zoteroPane.js`'s `setUpKeyboardNavigation` supplies the route instead: a
  hardcoded actions map keyed by exact element ids, handling Tab, Shift-Tab and
  arrows.
- `zotero-tb-note-add`'s `Tab` handler jumps unconditionally to
  `zotero-tb-search` and calls `preventDefault()` and `stopPropagation()`; its
  `ArrowNext` is a dead end.
- `sdt-pack-sitter-button` is appended after `zotero-tb-note-add` and appears
  nowhere in that map, under any id or class.

If that reading is right, the consequence is that tabbing forward through the
toolbar group jumps over our control to the search box, and Shift-Tab back from
the search box lands on `zotero-tb-note-add` rather than on us. The control
would then be focusable — programmatically, and to an assistive technology —
while still not being reachable by the keystroke a person would actually use.

**Focusable and reachable are different claims, and only the first is
demonstrated.** PR #554's release-note text was corrected to say so.

## What would settle it

One keypress on a focused window: put focus on `zotero-tb-note-add`, press Tab,
read `document.activeElement`. The blocker is the same one PR #554 recorded for
tab order generally — `windowUtils.sendKeyEvent` is gone in the Gecko 140 these
builds ship, `sendNativeKeyEvent` delivers nothing to a window without X focus,
and a dispatched Tab does not move focus. A session with a real focused window,
or `xdotool` on the host, settles both this and 0769's tab-order reading in the
same run.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `onMainWindowLoad`, where the control is
  built and where `tabindex="0"` was added
- `verification/probes/sdt_panel_keyboard.js` — the in-window assertions; its
  `tabMovesFocus` control is what reports tab order as NOT-ESTABLISHED
- `verification/probes/run_panel_keyboard.py` — the driver

## Actions

1. Take the reading above on a machine with a focused window.
2. If the control is indeed skipped, decide the remedy — and the decision is
   the work, not the edit. Writing this add-on's id into a map that belongs to
   Zotero's own pane script is not obviously ours to do; an approach that does
   not reach into the host's navigation table is preferable if one exists.

## Test

Whatever the remedy, the red step is the reading itself: focus the neighbouring
toolbar button, press Tab, and assert where focus lands. A test that asserts
only that the control CAN take focus is the test that already passes and is
exactly what missed this.

## Verification

- [ ] The tab reading taken on a focused window, naming the machine
- [ ] If a remedy lands, the same reading shown to have failed before it

## Invariants

- The control stays focusable: whatever this ticket concludes, removing
  `tabindex="0"` returns the panel to being mouse-only, which is the defect
  PR #554 fixed.
- Zotero's own toolbar conventions are the host's. A remedy that fights them,
  or that depends on the exact shape of `setUpKeyboardNavigation`, buys a
  reachable button and an unbounded maintenance debt against a file we do not
  own.

## Exit criteria

The question is answered by a reading rather than by inference, and either the
control is reachable by Tab or a ruling records why it is acceptable that it is
not. `plugins/sdt-sitter/RELEASE-NOTES.md` then says whichever is true.
